The answer is (A): When she stirred the tea, the whirlpool it created was spinning in the same direction as the original cup of tea.

Here's the explanation: Most cups are made of right-handed glass (meaning the spiral patterns on the glass are oriented clockwise). When you stir your tea in a clockwise direction, the whirlpool created also spins clockwise. The reason the woman knew the new tea was the same is that the whirlpool in the new cup was spinning in the same direction as the original cup, indicating it was from the same manufacturer and, therefore, the same cup.

This phenomenon is called the "handedness" of glass and is a result of the manufacturing process. During the glassblowing process, the molten glass is spun to create the spiral patterns, and the direction of the spin determines the handedness of the glass.

So, the woman's clever observation and knowledge of glass handedness led her to deduce that the new tea was indeed the same as the original!
